# The Dark Ages: an agent-played game of Dynasty. Scenario and steering: scenario.md; deck: seed.md.
# Every say and aside is the reply of the agent playing that seat (see log/yearN/).
# The Storyteller writes the act lines and refs the moves; the engine keeps the books.
# The narrator's lines are not part of the game: they live in narration.md, never here.
# Each chapter opens with a "# ===== CHAPTER N: <title>" marker.
setup([1, 2, 3, 5, 6, 8], "dark-ages", "The Dark Ages",
      "Six players · in progress",
      "Six players: the Emperor, the Official, the Gentry, the Silk and Rice Peasants, and the Warlord. "
      "Four good years before the population comes, and a crown in a hurry to build.",
      short="6 players")
REVOLT_LINE = True   # briefs.py puts the causes for revolt on every board card (the Director's choice for this game)

# ============================== SETUP ==============================
R(0, "Setup", None, "Six players: the Emperor, the Official, the Gentry, the Silk and Rice Peasants and the Warlord (rulebook Section 4 and Appendix A). No Scholar, so the Emperor appoints someone to read I Ching and count the population, and pays the appointee whatever she chooses (Note 3). Each realia province holds two pieces of its own realia, and each collector needs one from each of the other three. The Warlord starts with two soldiers and may hire one more, for 20, after any march (Section 14).")
act("Six booklets: Reina Osorio draws the Emperor, Clark Grafton the Official, Richard Baron the Gentry, Silke Weber the Silk Peasant, Paddy Doyle the Rice Peasant and Robert Land the Warlord.", map_hl=[1,2,3,5,6,8])
deal_grain("Rich, the Gentry, sets out the Harvest Pile and deals 60 to each player. It is the first year's harvest.")
take_stock("Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy each take two pieces of their own realia out of the box. They wait beside the board until they're sold.")
act("Clark cuts out the Province 2 Irrigation System, one square by two; Rich the Province 3 Reservoir, two by three. They're set aside until built. Nobody sits in Provinces 4 or 7, so nothing is owed there.", map_hl=[2,3])
# ===== CHAPTER 1: Agreeing Is Not Deposing

place_army({"W1":"G11", "W2":"Q26"}, "Rob, the Warlord, stands his two soldiers: W1 on G11, on the strip below the Wall where Clark's Province 2 meets Rich's Province 3, and W2 on Q26, in the sea off Silke's Province 5 and Paddy's Province 6. He refuses to pay the crown's tax.")
say("Rob", "Good evening, neighbours. One of my lads is camped under the Wall right where Clark's fields meet Rich's, and the other's on a boat off the coast, just about where Silke's silk meets Paddy's rice. No tax from me, Reina. Collect it yourself if you like, I'll have the kettle on.",
 "Rob: G11 touches Clark's and Rich's land at once, and Q26 out at sea touches both peasants, so each soldier is one step from two of the four pieces I need. Refusing the tax on day one tells the Empress early that she doesn't control me.")
act("Reina, the Emperor, will read the I Ching herself, unpaid, and shuffles the deck.", map_hl=[1])
say("Reina", "The Mandate comes from Heaven, so the crown will read Heaven's book itself. I'll shuffle the deck, turn the cards and keep the count, and nobody will need to wonder whose hand turned them. The first harvest will be a full one, so sharpen your sickles. The walls won't build themselves.",
 "Reina: There's no reason to pay someone grain to read a card I can read myself. Every grain the crown keeps goes to the 890 in works, and I want the foundations dug before the population card turns.")
# ============================== YEAR 1 ==============================
R(1, "The first year", None, "No card is drawn. The 60 each player was dealt is the first harvest, and the tax is levied on it.")
CUR_INC.update({s:60 for s in ACTIVE})
say("Rich", "Your Majesty, what a gracious thing, reading Heaven's book with your own hands. Nobody at this table could doubt a count you keep. Silke, Paddy, my dears, it's the customary five each, the smallest rent in the empire, and the Pile pays out the moment the sickles are in. And Rob, keep your kettle, and keep your boys off my side of the Wall.",
 "Rich: Five is a starting price. Ask too much in Year 1 and the peasants start talking about revolts, so I ask the minimum, look generous, and raise it later when they owe me a favour. My grain is the same as everyone's for now, and I'm watching Clark's Customs House, which I need, and Rob's soldier sitting right on my border.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich, the Gentry, asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum rent, and each pays him 5.")
say("Reina", "Half the harvest, friends. The fields will never be fuller than they are tonight, and an empire that waits for lean years to build will never build at all. The Summer Palace goes up this year, and the Wall and the Canal start from it. Clark, collect it cleanly and you'll have your five at year's end. Rob, keep your kettle. The crown doesn't knock on tents.",
 "Reina: At 50% the crown takes 120, which leaves the reserve at 155 after the Palace and Clark's wage, and each peasant still has 25 in hand, so nobody can claim to be in debt. The Summer Palace is the key to the Wall and the Canal, and I want both started before the population card turns. I'm watching Rich: his flattery is too smooth, and he's the one most likely to gather two friends for a revolt.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 50% on the first harvest: 30 each from Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy, 120 in all; Rob refuses his. The crown will build the Summer Palace, all 20 from the reserve, and pay Clark 5 as the Official at the year's end.", map_hl=[1])
say("Clark", "Thirty from each of us, a hundred and twenty to the crown, counted twice and written down once. Majesty, it's in the ledger: fifty percent in the first year, and the Summer Palace before a single stone of the Wall. And Rob, I've noted your refusal too, with the date. A warlord the crown can't tax is a warlord the crown can't control, and that's a page in my book now.",
 "Clark: I pay my thirty like a good servant, and the books stay spotless, so nobody can say I cheated. But a half-share tax in year one and a Warlord who won't pay are two causes for revolt waiting to come true. I'm keeping a note of who nods along when I read them out.")
taxes(.50, skip=(8,), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
build("Summer Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Summer Palace", at="G20", text="Public works: Reina builds the Summer Palace on G20, at the crown's cost of 20.", hover="Reina pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. The Summer Palace comes before the Great Wall and the Province 1 canal.")
say("Clark", "The Customs House starts at three, friends. It's an honest house and it keeps honest books. As for the rest, I'll bid like a clerk, small and steady. Rich, you already own the land under us all, so you don't need the jar and the bowl as well. I'll be writing down who bids against whom.",
 "Clark: The crown took half my grain, so I'm buying cheap pieces and keeping my powder dry, but I'll pay extra to keep a Jar or a Bowl out of the landlord's hands. A Gentry with a full set is a Gentry on the throne, and I'd sooner see the chair empty.")
say("Rich", "Majesty, a palace first. Very wise, a crown ought to have a roof before it has a wall. My rent's in, Silke, Paddy, thank you both, and the Harvest Pile is safe with me. As for tonight's trinkets, I'll bid like a gentleman: modestly, and I'll pay on the nail. And my deeds go no lower than three. Land is land.",
 "Rich: Everyone at this table is skint except the crown and the man in the tent. So I bid small and spread it over three lots, and keep enough back for the twenty grain the set needs. If I come out with one piece I'm the man the others have to deal with.")
say("Silke", "Let's count it out loud, shall we? Thirty to the crown, five to Rich, and the crown sits on a hundred and sixty while I sit on twenty-five. So I'll bid like a woman with twenty-five, and anyone who wants to run these prices up is just feeding the Pile. My Dragon Jar starts at three, and it's worth every grain.",
 "Silke: I want one of each piece cheap, and I'm not getting into a war over any of them, because three quarters of a war goes into the Pile. The only one I'll overpay for is the Bowl, and only if it keeps Rich from finishing a set off my rent.")
say("Paddy", "Well, I paid my thirty and my five and I've twenty-five left to my name, so I won't be running anyone's prices up tonight. I'll put in a fair bid on each piece and no more. And my Bowl goes up at two. It's a plain thing, and I'd rather it went to a good table than sat in my kitchen.",
 "Paddy: I need all three pieces and I can't afford a fight over any of them, so it's six apiece. If I win them all I've still got seven grain to my name and I owe nobody. If someone wants a piece badly enough to pay over six, they can have it, and three quarters of their grain goes to the Pile.")
say("Rob", "Go on, bid, all of you. Buy your pretty jars and bowls and scrolls and put them out on the board where I can see them. I don't need to own a thing, friends. I only need to know where you've put it. And Clark, write this in your book too: the Warlord kept his sixty, and the crown spent twenty on a summer house nine rounds from my tent.",
 "Rob: Every piece they buy tonight ends up on a square, and every square on the board is somewhere I can get to. I'm letting them pay full price for my targets while I keep my grain dry and walk the boys toward that palace.")
act("The auctions, in province order. Rob passes every lot.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
DS = "Take it, then. I can see who's counting and who's just spending."
DP = "Go on then, it's yours. I'll not go hungry over a jar."
DR = "Take it, take it, with my blessing. I'll know where to find it."
lot(2, "CH", [B(5,6), B(5,None,DS), B(6,6), B(6,None,DP), B(3,7)],
 hover="Clark's minimum was 3. Silke and Paddy stopped at 6; Rich's limit was 7. Rich puts the Customs House on L10.", at="L10")
lot(3, "LD", [B(5,5), B(5,None,DS), B(6,6), B(6,None,DP), B(2,6)],
 hover="Rich's minimum was 3. Silke stopped at 5. Paddy and Clark both went to 6, and a tie goes by seat order, to Clark. Clark puts the Land Deed Scroll on J14.", at="J14")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(3,6), B(3,None,DR), B(6,6), B(6,None,DP), B(2,7)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 3. Rich and Paddy stopped at 6; Clark's limit was 6, or 9 to keep a piece from Rich. Clark puts the Dragon Jar on J16.", at="J16")
lot(6, "WB", [B(3,6), B(3,None,DR), B(5,6), B(5,None,DS), B(2,7)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 2. Rich and Silke stopped at 6; Clark's limit was 6, or 9 to keep a piece from Rich. Clark puts the Wooden Bowl on I15, and holds a complete set.", at="I15")
say("Clark", "A scroll, a jar and a bowl, all in the Official's keeping and all paid for on the nail. Rob, since you asked so nicely, they're at the back of my province, behind the clerks, the granary and a great deal of paperwork. And Rich, the Customs House is yours. Try to keep honest books in it.",
 "Clark: That's a full set in year one, but I'm down to about ten grain and I need twenty in hand to use it, so the clever move now is to sit tight and look harmless. They're all in the southern corner, out of Rob's reach for two rounds, and by the time anyone counts my pieces I mean to have the grain to cash them in.")
say("Rich", "A Customs House! Thank you, Clark. Seven grain, and a quarter of it back in your pocket, so we're practically partners. I'll keep it well back from the Wall. No need to tempt our friend in the tent. And Clark, a full set in your first year. Congratulations. I'm sure you'll be writing down who you mean to replace with it.",
 "Rich: Clark has a whole set and only thirty grain, and the set needs twenty of that, so the Official is one good harvest from coming for somebody's chair. I've put my house deep in the south of my province where Rob can't reach it, and from tonight I'm counting who would stand with the crown if Clark moves.")
march({"W1":"H12", "W2":"P25"}, "Rob marches: W1 crosses the north river onto H12 in Clark's Province 2, paying Clark a 5 toll and raiding 5 from him; W2 comes ashore on P25 in Silke's Province 5, raiding 5 from her. He hires no one.")
say("Rob", "Clark, you can stack paperwork as high as the Wall and I'll still read it. Here's five for your river toll, and I'm taking five back for the walk. We'll call that our first transaction. Silke, sorry, love, the sea air makes me peckish. That's five off the coast, and nobody in this province ordered it except me.",
 "Rob: W1 crosses the river into Clark's country and the Land Deed's two steps off now, with the bowl and jar right behind it. W2 lands on the coast and starts the long walk inland toward that brand-new Summer Palace.")
salaries({2:5}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 5 as the Official.")

# ============================== YEAR 2 ==============================
R(2, "Rain and flood", "rainflood")
act("Reina turns the year's I Ching card: Spring Floods. Provinces 1, 3 and 6 collect a good harvest; Provinces 2 and 5 and the desert a bad one. The population holds at x1, 100 million.")
say("Reina", "Spring Floods. Heaven waters the land generously, and in some places far too generously. Provinces one, three, six and seven have a good harvest; two, four, five and eight have a bad one. The counter stands at one hundred million, the first multiple, so everyone reads the times-one row. Clark, Silke, I'm sorry about your fields. But the river rises for the whole empire, and the empire will still be standing when it goes down again.",
 "Reina: My own province drew a good harvest, and so did Rich's and Paddy's, so there will be plenty of grain to tax. Clark and Silke got the bad one, and they have the least to spare, so I'll be watching to see if either of them starts counting the votes for a revolt.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "The harvest's paid out to the grain, friends: Majesty forty, Clark ten, Silke ten, Paddy sixty, and sixty to me, since Heaven happened to smile on Province 3. Silke, my dear, after the floods and that sea-thief, it's the bare five from you and not a grain more. Paddy, Heaven gave you the same sixty it gave me, so I'll ask ten this year, with my thanks. A good year shares its luck.",
 "Rich: Silke is wet, robbed and angry, so I go easy on her, and that buys me a friend if Clark ever puts his set to work. Paddy is fat on rice and can spare the extra five, and if he grumbles, I'll take it gracefully back down to five and he'll remember I was reasonable.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich asks Silke for the minimum 5 and Paddy for 10. Each pays the minimum 5 now; anything over 5 is Paddy's to agree to.")
say("Reina", "Fifty percent again, friends, the same rate as last year, so nobody can say the crown keeps changing its mind. Fifty percent of a flooded field is only five grain, Clark, Silke, so the floods take care of their own; Rich and Paddy, Heaven smiled on you and the crown asks for its share of the smile. The Winter Palace goes up this year, and once it stands the Grand Canal can come to the coast. Clark gets his five at the year's end, as before.",
 "Reina: The tax lands on Rich and Paddy, who can afford it, and Clark and Silke pay five each, so the two with a grievance have very little to complain about. The Winter Palace is what the coast canals need before they can be built, so it gives Silke and Paddy a reason to want this crown to stay.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 50% for the second year running: 5 each from Clark and Silke, 30 each from Rich and Paddy, 70 in all; Rob refuses his. The crown will build the Winter Palace, all 20 from the reserve, and pay Clark 5 at the year's end.", map_hl=[1])
act("Before the Official collects, the Storyteller reads out a rules note to Silke, in front of the table: a revolt can be called at any step, by anyone, the Warlord included, if two others agree on a cause from the list on the board card. Once it's called with two agreeing, play stops until it stands or is dropped. The Emperor may answer it, and it stands only if the caller and two others still back it. One called now, before the tax is paid, voids this year's tax. Under Chaos nobody pays tax, salaries or works, and nobody can buy new land; the harvest, the rent, the auctions and the Warlord carry on. The reserve is sealed and passes whole to whoever is acclaimed. The throne then goes only by acclamation: anyone but the deposed Emperor may claim it, a peasant or the Warlord included, and the claimants are counted on grain in hand, soldiers on the board and friends.", map_hl=[5])
act("Clark, Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob are each asked at the same time, before the Official collects: pay the tax, or call a revolt and name the cause, or say they'd back one. Paddy is also asked whether he'll pay Rich the extra 5 of rent.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6,8])
say("Clark", "Five grain from a flooded field, Majesty, and the Official pays first so nobody can say the collector skimped. But it's going in the book: fifty percent two years running, and a Warlord who raids the crown's own collector and pays nothing. Write 'consistently' next to 'excessive' and that's a revolt with my name on it. Not tonight. Tonight I collect.",
 "Clark: A revolt now would bring Chaos, and under Chaos my full set can't take the throne, so I'd be throwing away the best hand at the table. I pay my five, keep my twenty in hand, and keep the grievances on file in case I need a mob later.")
say("Rich", "Thirty, Majesty, and gladly. Heaven smiled on Province 3 and Province 3 smiles on the crown. A Winter Palace beside the Summer one, very fitting. Though I'll mention, purely as a loyal subject, that the man in the tent has now eaten twice from our larders and paid the crown nothing. Fifty percent is easier to love when everyone pays it.",
 "Rich: The crown has a fat reserve and Reina still owns the table, so I pay and I bow. But I've said aloud that she can't control the Warlord, and that's one of the causes on the card. If the wind turns, everyone will remember who said it first, and said it politely.")
say("Silke", "Let's count it, Majesty. I paid thirty for your Summer Palace, and Rob walked off my beach with five while your soldiers sat at home. Now it's five more for a Winter Palace I'll never see the inside of. If someone at this table says the crown can't control the Warlord, I'll say it too, because my coast already says it.",
 "Silke: Five grain won't ruin me, but nobody is going to tax me for palaces while the Warlord raids me for free. I'm not calling it myself, but if two others stand up, so will I, and then Reina has to buy my backing back.")
say("Paddy", "Thirty to the crown, and I'll pay it, Majesty. Fifty in a good year is steep, but you're putting up stone with it and I can see the stone. Rich, you'll have your five, same as Silke, and thank you for asking kindly. Heaven gave you the same sixty it gave me, so let's leave Heaven's luck with whoever it fell on.",
 "Paddy: I'm not calling a revolt over two years of fifty percent while the palaces actually get built. That's a hard tax, but not an unjust one yet. The extra five to Rich is another matter: he's sitting on the same sixty as me and the whole Harvest Pile besides, and I'm saving every grain for a set after losing all three pieces at the auction by a single grain.")
say("Rob", "I'll say it, since nobody else will. Two years now I've refused her tax, I've walked across her rivers and taken grain off her coast, and the crown's answer is a second palace. Your Majesty can't control the Warlord. Clark wrote it in his own ledger. Silke, Clark, back me and none of you pays a grain this year.",
 "Rob: There's no cause on that card I like better than the one that's about me. If two of them sign it, the tax is void, the throne's empty, and the man with the soldiers gets counted at acclamation.")
say("Clark", "Rob, quoting my ledger back at me is very flattering, but a revolt called by the man who caused the grievance goes in the book under 'cheek'. I won't be the third hand that hands the empire to the tent. Majesty, you'll notice the Official held the line, and you'll notice it's written down. A loyal collector is worth more than five a year.",
 "Clark: Chaos would freeze my full set, and Rob would be the strongest man in an empty room, so I'm not helping him. Saying no costs me nothing, and it gives me a favour to call in with Reina, starting with a bigger salary.")
say("Rich", "Majesty, I said it before Rob did, and more politely: twice he has eaten from our larders and twice the crown has built a palace. So yes, I agree the cause exists. But agreeing is not deposing. The card says the crown may answer, and I'm a very reasonable man. Show us a plan for the Warlord, and perhaps a gentler rate on the subjects Heaven smiled on, and Province 3 will stand where it has always stood, beside the throne.",
 "Rich: Now it's called and the whole table stops for Reina, and my vote is the one that keeps it standing or lets it fall. I don't want Chaos with Rob's soldiers counting toward the throne. I want the crown to pay me to walk back to its side.")
say("Paddy", "I said I'd pay, and I'll pay. Rob, you're right that the crown hasn't stopped you, but I'm not taking advice on that from the fellow doing the raiding. Your boat's sitting one square off my rice fields. Silke, I'm sorry about your beach, truly. Majesty, it'd be a kindness if some of that reserve went toward soldiers on the coast before the next palace.",
 "Paddy: A revolt Rob calls is a revolt that hands Rob the board. Under Chaos the throne goes on grain and soldiers, and he's the one with soldiers. Province 6 isn't fed by the man standing on its doorstep, so I'm keeping my word, and I'm putting the crown on notice in public.")
revolt_called("Rob calls a revolt before the tax is collected, on the cause 'the Emperor can't control the Warlord'. Silke and Rich agree; Clark and Paddy don't. With two agreeing, the revolt is called, and play stops until it stands or is dropped. Reina may answer it.")
say("Reina", "Very well, Rob, you've been heard, and the crown doesn't need to be asked twice. The Winter Palace can wait a year. Forty grain from the reserve buys two of your own soldiers, at the price you're obliged to accept: one for Silke on her beach and one for Clark on his river, in the two provinces you've already raided, so both can march today. The rate stays where it is, because a crown that haggles over its tax gets asked to haggle again next year. Rich, you asked for a plan, and here it is: the Warlord gets met with his own men. And next year the crown puts forty toward your Reservoir before it builds anything else for itself.",
 "Reina: I'm giving up a building, not cutting the tax, and every grain of it goes to the two coasts that were actually raided, so Silke can't complain that she was left unprotected. Rich is the vote that decides this, and a reservoir in his province is worth more to him than a few points off the rate.")
act("Reina answers the revolt. The tax stays at 50%, and the Winter Palace is put off a year. The crown pays Silke and Clark 20 each from the reserve, to buy a soldier each from Rob for Provinces 5 and 2, which have been raided, so those soldiers can move. She promises the Province 3 Reservoir in Year 3, ahead of any palace, with the crown paying half its 80.", deltas={pid(1):-40, pid(5):20, pid(2):20}, map_hl=[1,2,3,5], deal=True)
say("Rob", "Your Majesty, that's the most generous thing anyone's ever done for me. You've answered 'you can't control the Warlord' by paying the Warlord forty grain. I'll sell those two lads, I have to, and I'll still be standing by my call. Silke, Rich, have a think about who just got paid for this.",
 "Rob: Either way I win. If it stands, the throne's empty and I'm in the count with soldiers on the board, and if they fold I've still got forty of the crown's grain for two boys I can hire back. Now let's see if the gentleman's word is worth more than his manners.")
say("Silke", "Twenty grain and a soldier on my own beach. That's the first time this crown has spent anything on my coast, Majesty, and I'll take it back for that. But count with me: a revolt today hands the throne to whoever has the most grain and soldiers, and that's the man who's been raiding me. I'm not crowning my own thief. Rob, your boat leaves my sand or my soldier meets it.",
 "Silke: I made Reina bid for my backing and she paid twenty grain for it, which is a better deal than Chaos with Rob counting soldiers at the acclamation. The grievance stays on the table, though. If that soldier doesn't hold the coast, I'll be the one calling the next revolt.")
say("Rich", "Majesty, that is a plan, and a generous one. Rob's own soldiers sent against him, and the Reservoir for Province 3 next year, half paid by the crown. I said I'd be beside the throne if you answered, and you've answered, so here I am. I withdraw, and I'll pay my thirty now. Rob, no hard feelings. You did make an excellent point, and the crown has just bought two of your men with it.",
 "Rich: One word from me and I got the Warlord answered, a Reservoir half paid for, and Silke armed, all without risking Chaos with Rob's soldiers in the count. Everyone at the table now knows the Gentry's vote can be bought, and that once it's bought it stays bought.")
revolt_dropped("Silke and Rich take the revolt back after Reina's answer; only Rob stands by it. The revolt is dropped, and play resumes.")
taxes(.50, skip=(8,), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
act("Reina puts the Winter Palace off a year, as she answered: nothing is built this year.", map_hl=[1])
say("Clark", "My last Customs House goes up at four, and it's a bargain at that. I'm sitting the rest out, friends. The Official already has everything he needs, including, I'm pleased to say, a soldier on order courtesy of the crown. And Rich, a word in public: you agreed to that revolt, then withdrew it once a Reservoir was promised. That's in the book too, under 'price of loyalty: eighty grain.'",
 "Clark: That's the last Wooden Bowl in Paddy's stock, and Rich needs it to finish his set, so if he reaches for it I'll overpay to take it off the board for good. I'll pass on the rest and hold my grain, twenty for the soldier and twenty to cash in my set.")
say("Rich", "Friends, let's not feed the Pile out of spite. Every piece left is the last of its kind. Silke, Paddy, you each need the Customs House and something else, and there's only one Customs House, so one of you will be short whatever happens. Let me have the Jar and the Bowl quietly and I won't lift a finger on the Customs House. Bid against me and we all end up poorer, and Rob ends up laughing.",
 "Rich: These are the last Jar and the last Bowl, and with my Customs House they make a set, which makes me the man who can unseat anybody, Clark included. I'll pay up to twenty-two each and still keep the twenty grain the set needs.")
say("Silke", "Forty-one and a half, friends, and twenty of it is the crown's soldier, so I'm bidding with twenty-one and a half and not a grain more. Paddy, we want the same two pieces, so don't let the Pile eat us both. And Rich, you've seventy-four and a half and you're one bowl short of a set. I can count that too.",
 "Silke: The soldier money stays untouched, and I've split the rest so I can afford all three lots in the worst case. The Bowl matters most: if Rich finishes his set, it gives him a lever over every peasant at the table, me included.")
say("Paddy", "Last of each on the table, so I'll say it plain: I mean to have all three this year, and I'll pay fair for them, not foolish. Silke, you and I want the same two pieces, and there's only one of each. If we run each other up, the Pile eats it and neither of us gets fed. My Bowl goes up at two again. Somebody give it a good home.",
 "Paddy: I can spend thirty-one and still keep the twenty a set needs, so that's my whole purse across the three lots, with a grain extra on the Customs House because whoever gets it is the only one of us who can finish. If Silke outbids me, it costs her nearly everything she has, and I'll keep my grain and wait.")
say("Rob", "No hard feelings, Rich? None at all. You stood beside the throne, and you'll find I'm very fond of people who stand still. Bid away, everyone. Silke, mind your soldier, he used to be mine and he still knows the way home.",
 "Rob: Rich is one jar and one bowl short of a set, and a gentleman holding a set gets ideas about whose chair he'd like. I'll pay a little to make sure the only jar on sale tonight sits on a shelf in my tent.")
act("The auctions, in province order.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6])
DS = "Fine. I'd rather keep the grain than make the Pile fat."
DP = "Fair enough, it's yours. I'll not go into debt for a scroll."
DC = "Noted, price and buyer both."
DB = "Keep it, then. I know where you'll put it."
lot(2, "CH", [B(5,6), B(5,None,DS), B(6,7)],
 hover="Clark's minimum was 4. Silke stopped at 6; Paddy's limit was 11. Paddy puts the Customs House on S19.", at="S19")
lot(3, "LD", [B(5,6), B(5,None,DS), B(6,7)],
 hover="Rich's minimum was 4. Silke stopped at 6; Paddy's limit was 10. Paddy puts the Land Deed Scroll on S20.", at="S20")
lot(5, "DJ", [B(8,9), B(8,None,DB), B(6,10), B(6,None,DP), B(3,11)],
 hover="Silke's minimum was 3. Rob would go to 9 only to keep it from Rich; Paddy stopped at 10; Rich's limit was 22. Rich puts the Dragon Jar on M12.", at="M12")
lot(6, "WB", [B(5,9.5), B(5,None,DS), B(2,10), B(2,None,DC), B(3,11)],
 hover="Paddy's minimum was 2. Silke went to 9.5 and Clark to 10, each only to keep it from Rich; Rich's limit was 22. Rich puts the Wooden Bowl on L14, and holds a complete set.", at="L14")
say("Paddy", "Thank you, Clark, Rich. Seven each and paid on the nail. They're going right at the back of the rice, as far from the sea as Province 6 goes. Rich, congratulations on the set, and on paying eleven for my Bowl. I'll not complain about that one.",
 "Paddy: That's two of the three I need, and both tucked away where Rob's boat can't reach for a good while. The trouble is every Dragon Jar is now in Clark's hands or Rich's, so from here a set means a trade or a bit of luck, and I'll be watching which of them gets hungry first.")
say("Rich", "A Jar and a Bowl, and both at a gentleman's price. Thank you, Silke, thank you, Paddy, a quarter of it is in your pockets. I'll keep them in the deep south of my province, beside the Customs House, well away from anyone's old soldiers. And Clark, write it down properly: the price of loyalty was eighty grain, and the crown paid it to the province that feeds you all.",
 "Rich: There are two full sets on the board now, Clark's and mine, and every face at this table just did the same sum. I don't mean to use mine yet. A set held back is a set every claimant has to court, and I'd rather be courted than crowned.")
say("Silke", "The crown paid twenty for a soldier on my coast, and the crown gets one. Twenty to Rob, five for his pocket and fifteen for the Pile, and he stands right next to Rob's boat. And Rich, count with me: one set at the table, and it's yours. I won't forget whose rent paid for it.",
 "Silke: There are no Customs Houses left, so my set is gone and I've got nothing to bid with but grain and nerve. That makes this soldier my only real leverage, so he goes nose to nose with Rob's boat, ready to take it the moment he can move.")
say("Clark", "The crown's twenty, spent as the crown intended. One soldier, standing guard over the Official's Land Deed. Rob, your lad on H12 can admire it from a distance. And let the minutes show that the Gentry now holds a full set, bought at eleven and eleven while he asked everyone else to keep the bidding down. I outbid him on nothing, and it's all written down.",
 "Clark: Rich has his set now, and I lost the Bowl to him by a single grain, so my set has to survive or he has a free run. The soldier sits on the Deed, the piece Rob's closest to. Next year he can march, and Rob's man on H12 is close enough to catch.")
march({"W1":"I13", "W2":"Q24"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto I13 in Clark's Province 2, raiding 5 from him; W2 steps off the coast onto Q24 in Paddy's Province 6, raiding 5 from him. He hires no one.")
say("Rob", "Clark, Silke, enjoy your new lads, I trained them myself. Clark, another five off the Official, and my boy's sitting two squares from that bowl you were so proud of. Paddy, you said Province 6 goes back a long way from the sea. Let's find out how far.",
 "Rob: W1 skirts round Clark's new guard and lines up on both the Bowl and Rich's pieces to the east. W2 slips off Silke's beach before her soldier arrives and starts walking toward Paddy's two pieces in the south.")
sell_soldiers([(5,"P24"), (2,"J14")], "Soldiers: with the crown's 20, Silke buys a soldier from Rob and stands it on P24, and Clark buys one and stands it on J14, on his Land Deed. Rob keeps 5 of each; 15 of each goes to the Harvest Pile.")
salaries({2:5}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 5 as the Official.")

# ===== CHAPTER 2: Paid in Full and in Public

# ============================== YEAR 3 ==============================
R(3, "Careless prosperity", "careless")
act("At the start of the year, before the I Ching, the Storyteller reads out a rules note to Paddy, in front of the table: here is how an acclamation would count if it were held tonight with Rob claiming. Rob has 85 grain, two soldiers and, so far, no friends. Silke and Clark each have a soldier, and anyone may buy one from Rob for 20. Rob would lead in soldiers. A rival who leads in grain, or in friends, leads a count too, and then nobody leads more counts than the other, so Chaos goes on: no tax for anyone until the table settles. To be acclaimed, Rob needs to lead in two of the three, and that means friends. A revolt crowns whoever the table backs, not whoever owns the most soldiers.", map_hl=[6])
act("Reina turns the year's I Ching card: Careless Prosperity. Every province collects a good harvest, and every player plays the next Personal Disaster in their booklet. The population holds at x1, 100 million.")
say("Reina", "Careless Prosperity. Good times make careless people. Every province brings in a good harvest, and every one of us plays a Personal Disaster, the crown included. The counter reads one hundred million, so the multiple is still x1: read the x1 row of your income tables. A full harvest, friends. Don't spend it all at the market, and don't start crying over this year's tax before you've counted your grain.",
 "Reina: A good harvest across the board is exactly the year a crown should take its share. The Winter Palace has waited long enough. The line about careless people is for whoever spends his windfall on trinkets instead of on me, and I'm looking at Rich.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
disaster(1, "the crown pays 4.")
disaster(2, "Clark pays 4.")
disaster(3, "Rich pays 5.")
disaster(5, "Silke pays 4.")
disaster(6, "Paddy pays 4.")
disaster(8, "Rob's soldiers may not raid in Provinces 5, 6 or 7 this year.")
say("Rich", "The Pile pays out, friends, and what a year to pay it! Her Majesty forty, sixty apiece to Clark, Silke, Paddy and my humble self, twenty to our friend in the tent. Silke, Paddy, the customary five and not a grain more. A fat harvest is no excuse for a fat landlord, whatever certain people may be writing down.",
 "Rich: Five from a peasant with ninety in hand is nothing, and that's the point: if this table ever comes down to counting friends, I want both of them remembering the year the Gentry could have squeezed and didn't. My set's complete, so I can afford to look generous.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum rent, and each pays him 5.")
say("Reina", "Fifty percent, the same as every year, and in a year as fat as this nobody can call it hard. Last year I gave my word on the Province 3 Reservoir. Rich, the crown pays forty of the eighty, as promised. Put in your forty and it's dug this season. Clark, your five, with the crown's thanks.",
 "Reina: Keeping the reservoir promise leaves Rich no grievance he can name in public, and holding the rate at fifty in a good harvest still fills the reserve. The Winter Palace waits one more year, and next year it gets built.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 50% for the third year running: 30 each from Clark, Rich, Silke and Paddy, 120 in all; Rob refuses his. The crown will build the Province 3 Reservoir, paying 40 of its 80 if Rich puts in the other 40, and pay Clark 5 at the year's end.", map_hl=[1,3])
act("Clark, Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob are each asked at the same time, before the Official collects: pay the tax, or call a revolt and name the cause, or say they'd back one. Rich is also asked whether he'll put 40 into the Reservoir.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6,8])
say("Clark", "Fifty percent for the third year running, Your Majesty. Three is a pattern, and a pattern is a cause; I've underlined it twice. But a cause only has to be written down, it doesn't have to be called. Thirty from me, and I'll collect thirty apiece from Rich, Silke and Paddy, counted twice and written down once. Rob, your refusal goes on its own page again, next to 'the crown can't control the Warlord'.",
 "Clark: I pay because a revolt means Chaos, and under Chaos my complete set is worth nothing, while paid up I still have forty-nine grain and the twenty it takes to cash it in. Every year of fifty percent goes in the book, so when I take that chair, nobody can say I didn't warn them.")
say("Rich", "Thirty to the crown, gladly, and forty for the Reservoir. Your Majesty kept her word to the grain, and the Gentry keeps his. Clark, write that down too: Rich was bought last spring, and he stayed bought.",
 "Rich: Seventy out still leaves me forty-nine, well over the twenty my set needs, and the Reservoir gives me twenty a year for as long as I hold Province 3, so it's paid for itself in two harvests. A throne that keeps its promises is worth propping up, for now.")
say("Silke", "Fifty percent, three years running. That's ninety from my coast, and this year's thirty goes to dig Rich's reservoir, while I'm still guarding my own beach with a soldier I paid for myself. The crown sits on two hundred and fifty-six. Paddy, you've paid the same ninety I have. Clark, Rich, count yours too. Reina, I said last year my backing would cost you more. Here's the bill: bring the tax down, or I take this to the table.",
 "Silke: I told you I'd call it the moment that tax looked consistent, and three years at fifty is as consistent as it gets. I don't need it to stand, though. I need Reina to bid against it, and this time twenty grain won't cover it.")
say("Paddy", "Thirty it is, Clark, and count it in front of me, if you don't mind. In a year like this I'll not cry over the tax, Your Majesty. But that's a reservoir for Province 3 now, and I'm still waiting to hear what the crown means to build on the coast.",
 "Paddy: Heaven gave us a fat year and Rob can't come south this season, so there's nothing to revolt over. You don't pull the roof down the year it finally stops leaking. I'm paying, I'm keeping the rest close, and I'm watching whether Rich ever wants to part with that Dragon Jar.")
say("Rob", "Fifty percent, three years running, and the moment the harvest's fat she calls it fair. That isn't a tax, that's a habit. Clark, Rich, Silke, Paddy: it's your sixty and her thirty, count it up. I pay nothing either way, so I'm only asking for you lot. Back me and keep your grain.",
 "Rob: Last year she bought them back for a Reservoir and twenty apiece. Let's see how much it costs her this year to make a tax look fair, and every grain she pays out is a grain the crown hasn't got when I come for Province 1.")
act("Clark, Rich and Paddy will pay, and Rich agrees to put 40 into the Reservoir. Silke calls a revolt on the cause 'taxes are consistently excessive', and Rob, at the same moment, names the same cause. A revolt needs its caller and two others, so one more is needed. The answers crossed, so Clark, Rich and Paddy are each asked once whether they agree. For the record: Silke has paid the crown 65 in tax over the three years with this year's 30, not 90 (Paddy has paid 90), and her soldier was bought with the crown's 20.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6,8])
say("Clark", "Does the cause exist? Silke, it's in my book, three years and underlined. But a revolt now hands the crown to whoever's got the most soldiers and the loudest friends, and I don't see a reformer in that tent. So no, not tonight. Reina, write this down too: the Official held the line twice, and next year he'll want paying for it.",
 "Clark: Chaos would freeze my set, and right now my set is worth a throne, so the tax stays on file and the revolt stays off the table. Agreeing that a cause exists costs nothing to say. Calling it would cost me everything, so I'm leaving that to people with less to lose.")
say("Rich", "Silke, my dear, I was bought last spring and I stay bought. Her Majesty paid her forty on the day she said she would, and I won't call that unjust a week after taking it. Besides, a revolt means Chaos, and Chaos means nobody digs anything, on my side of the Wall or on yours.",
 "Rich: Chaos would stop my Reservoir, and under Chaos my complete set is worth nothing because the throne goes by acclamation, so the last thing I want is Rob counting soldiers. Paddy's the one to watch now: if he agrees, I'll find out what Reina pays to make it go away.")
say("Paddy", "I've paid ninety, Silke, and you've paid sixty-five, so don't spend my ninety for me. I said thirty to Clark a minute ago and I meant it. In a year this fat I'll not pull the roof down, and I'll not do it on Rob's say-so either. But Your Majesty, three years at half is a long time. Something built on the coast would go a long way toward my not agreeing next year.",
 "Paddy: Silke's offering me half a bribe for a revolt that puts Rob's soldiers in the counting, and I don't sell my word for the crown's loose change. Still, I've let Reina hear the coast is keeping score.")
act("Clark, Rich and Paddy don't agree. With only Rob beside her, Silke's call fails: a revolt needs its caller and two others. Play goes on.", map_hl=[5,8])
taxes(.50, skip=(8,), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Rich", "Right in the heart of Province 3, Your Majesty, K11 to L13, where everyone can admire the crown's generosity. My thanks to the throne, and to Clark for counting so carefully.",
 "Rich: That water now sits squarely between Rob's soldier on I13 and all three of my pieces, so any raid coming for my set either goes the long way round or pays me five to wade across. A reservoir that earns twenty a year and also works as a moat is the best forty I've ever spent.")
build("Province 3 Reservoir", 80, {1:40, 3:40}, key="res3", at="K11:L13", text="Public works: the Province 3 Reservoir is dug on K11 to L13, 40 from the crown and 40 from Rich, as Reina promised last year.", hover="The 80 goes to the Harvest Pile. Province 3's harvest rises by 20 from next year.")
say("Rob", "Three agreed it was excessive and three paid it anyway. I admire that, really I do. Clark, five from Province 2, and that's my choice, not the crown's. Your Bowl's next door now, so do keep an eye on it. Paddy, Silke, sleep well this year. The heavens have tied my hands on the coast, not Her Majesty.",
 "Rob: W1 takes five from Clark and stops right beside his Wooden Bowl on I15, so next round it's mine unless he pulls his soldier off the Deed to guard it. W2 sits four rounds from Paddy's Deed and waits out the disaster, and next time Silke calls a revolt I'll be first to agree.")
march({"W1":"I14"}, "Rob marches: W1 steps onto I14 in Clark's Province 2, raiding 5 from him, beside Clark's Wooden Bowl on I15; W2 stands on Q24, since his disaster forbids raids on the coast this year. He hires no one.")
say("Clark", "Rob, you've raided the Official four times now, and I have every one of them dated. Consider this the receipt. Clark1 steps onto I14, and your man leaves the board. Next to my Bowl is a bad place to stand.",
 "Clark: That soldier was one step from my Deed and my Bowl, and now he's gone, which leaves Rob one man short and my set still whole. I've been waiting two years to catch him, so I'm not giving it a third.")
say("Silke", "Sixty-five, Paddy, fair enough, I'll count my own grain right. But here's something I can count: one boat-thief standing next to my soldier with his hands tied. Rob, you said sleep well. I will. Your man won't.",
 "Silke: Nobody would back me in council, so I'm settling it on the beach. That's one of Rob's two soldiers gone off the coast, and the crown's own twenty paid for the soldier that did it.")
troops({"Clark1":"I14", "Silke1":"Q24"}, "Soldiers: Clark's soldier steps from J14 onto I14 and captures Rob's W1; Silke's steps from P24 onto Q24 and captures W2. Rob's army is off the board, 0 of 3. He may hire one soldier a year, for 20.")
salaries({2:5}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 5 as the Official.")

# ============================== YEAR 4 ==============================
R(4, "The treasure chest", "chest")
act("Reina turns the year's I Ching card: Imperial Charity. Every province collects an average harvest, and the crown gives every player 2 from the reserve. The population holds at x1, 100 million.")
say("Reina", "Imperial Charity. The Emperor, moved by the suffering of her people, opens her purse. Slightly. An average harvest in every province, and the crown pays two to each of you from the reserve. The counter still reads one hundred million, so the multiple is x1: read the x1 row. Silke, that's two from the treasury you called excessive. Spend it well.",
 "Reina: Heaven has the crown handing out alms the year after a tax revolt, and I'll take that gladly, because ten grain buys a lot of goodwill. Clark and Paddy both told me their price tonight, the Official's pay and something on the coast, so the Winter Palace goes up this year and opens the coastal canals.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
act("Imperial Charity: Reina pays 2 from the reserve to each of Clark, Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob.", deltas={pid(1):-10, pid(2):2, pid(3):2, pid(5):2, pid(6):2, pid(8):2}, map_hl=[1])
say("Rich", "An average year, friends, and the Pile pays out: fifteen to Her Majesty, thirty-five each to Clark, Silke and Paddy, ten to Rob, and fifty-five to me, with the Reservoir's twenty in it, thanks to the crown. Silke, Paddy, the customary five again. I've never squeezed a peasant in a lean year and I won't start in an ordinary one.",
 "Rich: Rob's off the board, my set is safe, and the crown's Reservoir has just paid me its first twenty, so this is the year to stay cheap and well liked. Two peasants who've paid me five every year will remember that if the table ever comes down to counting friends.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum rent, and each pays him 5.")
say("Reina", "Fifty percent. I said it would stay the same so nobody could say the crown keeps changing its mind, and it stays the same. This year the Winter Palace goes up, the one I put off two years ago to pay for your soldiers. Clark, you said the Official would want paying. Fifteen this year, because a man who counts this carefully has earned more than the minimum.",
 "Reina: I won't cut the rate for a revolt that already failed. The Winter Palace is my own monument, and it's also the foundation the coastal canals need, so Paddy can see something being built that points his way. Tripling Clark's pay costs me ten grain and keeps the man with the ledger on my side of it.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 50% for the fourth year running: 17.5 each from Clark, Silke and Paddy and 27.5 from Rich, 80 in all; Rob refuses his. The crown will build the Winter Palace, all 20 from the reserve, and pay Clark 15 at the year's end.", map_hl=[1])
act("Clark, Rich, Silke, Paddy and Rob are each asked at the same time, before the Official collects: pay the tax, or call a revolt and name the cause, or say they'd back one.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6,8])
say("Clark", "Fifteen. Your Majesty, that's the first raise this office has seen, and I've written it down in ink. Seventeen and a half from me, and I'll collect twenty-seven and a half from Rich and seventeen and a half each from Silke and Paddy, counted in front of you all. Four years at half is in the book too, under 'consistently'. I'm just not underlining it tonight.",
 "Clark: Paying keeps the throne in play for my set. With Rob's army off the board and eighty-six grain in hand, I'm in no hurry to start the Chaos that would freeze it. Reina has started paying me for my loyalty, so I keep my causes on file and let the price go up.")
say("Rich", "Twenty-seven and a half, Clark, count it twice. Half of the crown's own Reservoir comes back to the crown, and I call that a fair bargain. Fifteen for the Official, though, Your Majesty? Generous. Some of us have kept the line for years at no charge at all, and some of us hold a full set and simply choose to sit still.",
 "Rich: I'm paying, but I want the throne to remember that the only complete set at this table that isn't Clark's is in my hands, and he's just been paid fifteen to sit on his. Loyalty ought to cost the crown at least as much from me as it does from him.")
say("Silke", "Four years at half, and this year's half buys a Winter Palace. Twenty from a reserve of three hundred and thirty, so I'll never sit in it and the crown will hardly notice it. Clark got his raise, Rich got his reservoir, and the coast got two grain of charity. Paddy, you asked what the crown means to build on the coast. Here's your answer: a palace in the north. If anyone calls it, I'm standing behind them.",
 "Silke: I'm not calling it myself this time. A second call that fails just makes me look shrill. Whether it stands now depends on Paddy finally noticing he isn't getting his harbour.")
say("Paddy", "Seventeen and a half, Clark, and I'll watch you count it. Your Majesty, I asked last year for something on the coast, and the answer's a Winter Palace and a raise for the Official. That's four years at half. I'm paying this one, but write it down, Clark: if the fifth year comes and there's still nothing built south of Silke's beach, Paddy agrees the cause exists.",
 "Paddy: Rob's got no soldiers left, so a revolt wouldn't hand him the throne anymore, and that makes my threat worth something. I'm giving Reina one more year, and saying it out loud so she can't claim she wasn't told.")
say("Rob", "Well played, both of you, I mean it. Not a soldier of mine left on the board, and the crown still wants half your grain. Four years at fifty, and this year it builds her a Winter Palace. Paddy, you said something on the coast would buy you off. Look around: it's a palace in the north. Silke, you know the way. I'm only asking the table to count.",
 "Rob: With no soldiers I can't bully anyone this year, so I'll make Reina pay for every vote instead. Paddy as good as promised to agree if the coast got nothing, and it got nothing.")
act("Clark, Rich and Paddy will pay. Rob calls a revolt on the cause 'taxes are consistently excessive', and Silke backs it. A revolt needs its caller and two others, so one more is needed. The answers crossed, so Clark, Rich and Paddy are each asked once whether they agree.", map_hl=[2,3,5,6,8])
say("Clark", "Rob, a man with no soldiers on the board calling a revolt is like a man with no grain calling for an audit. It's bold, and I've filed it under 'cheek', page three. The cause is real, Silke, and it's in my book. But I'll be the one to say when it gets called, and it won't be on the Warlord's word.",
 "Clark: Chaos would freeze my set and leave the crown to a vote counted on soldiers and friends, and Rob's been buying friends with other people's grievances. A throne I can take with a set I already hold beats a throne the table might give someone else.")
say("Rich", "Does the cause exist? Four years at half. Clark has it in his book, so I can hardly say it doesn't. I agree it exists, Rob, and agreeing is not deposing. Your Majesty, I was bought for one spring and I stayed bought, every grain of it. This is a new spring, and the Gentry's price is posted, as ever: make me whole on my forty for the Reservoir and I'll step back as quickly as I stepped forward.",
 "Rich: With three hundred in the treasury the crown can pay me easily, and Rob has no soldier on the board to win a count with, so this is leverage, not a gamble. If Reina pays I drop out and the revolt falls; if she won't, I'll find out how much the crown values the one other set at this table.")
say("Paddy", "Rob, you were listening, so you heard me give the crown one more year, and I gave it out loud. I don't take my word back the same evening because the fellow who raided my front field likes the sound of a count. Your Majesty, the clock's running. Next year I'll be the one asking whether the cause exists, and I won't need Rob to ask for me.",
 "Paddy: Silke's offer is a good one, and next year I might take her up on it, but I said one more year and Paddy Doyle doesn't go back on his word inside a quarter of an hour. I'd rather the revolt be mine and the coast's when it comes than Rob's.")
revolt_called("Rob calls a revolt before the tax is collected, on the cause 'taxes are consistently excessive'. Silke and Rich agree; Clark and Paddy don't. With two agreeing, the revolt is called, and play stops until it stands or is dropped. Reina may answer it.")
say("Reina", "Rich, you posted your price, and the crown pays its debts in public. Forty grain, your whole share of the Reservoir, back in your hand, so it's my gift to Province 3 now. The rate stays at fifty. I won't cut it on the word of a man with no soldiers on the board and no grain in my treasury. A crown that bends whenever someone complains will snap, and none of you wants to stand under it when it does.",
 "Reina: Forty grain out of three hundred and thirty-six buys the one vote this revolt can't do without, and the rate never moves. Paddy has given me one year and said so out loud, so the dangerous one now is Rich: he has learned that his vote has a price, and he'll come back to collect it.")
act("Reina answers the revolt. She pays Rich 40 from the reserve, his whole share of the Province 3 Reservoir returned. The tax stays at 50%.", deltas={pid(1):-40, pid(3):40}, map_hl=[1,3], deal=True)
say("Rob", "I'm standing by it, of course. Forty to Rich and not a grain off the rate for anyone else. Silke, that answer was for him, not for you. Rich, you've been paid twice for the same Reservoir now. Lovely work if you can get it. Let's see whether it buys you twice.",
 "Rob: Rich will almost certainly fold again with forty fresh grain in his pocket, but I lose nothing by standing, and everyone watching saw the crown buy him off for a second time. If he holds, I'm the richest claimant at a table with no Emperor, and that's exactly where I want to be.")
say("Silke", "Forty grain to Rich and not one to the coast. Your Majesty, you bought the landlord again and forgot the woman with the only soldier on this board. I'm still standing. Rich, you'll have to take it back yourself, in front of everyone, with her forty in your hand.",
 "Silke: Reina paid the wrong backer, so I lose nothing by holding firm. If Rich folds, he folds where the whole table can watch. If he doesn't, the reserve gets sealed and my soldier is the only army left on the board.")
say("Rich", "Paid in full and in public. Your Majesty is a woman of her word, and so am I. I take it back, Rob, just as I said I would. Clark, enter it beside last year's: the Gentry's price was posted, paid and honoured, every grain.",
 "Rich: That's forty grain back, so the Reservoir cost me nothing and earns me twenty a year for good, and my set is still worth something because no Chaos has frozen the throne. Twice now the crown has paid me to stay, and Rob and Silke have learned exactly whose vote decides these things.")
revolt_dropped("Rich takes the revolt back after Reina's answer; Rob and Silke stand by it. With only two behind it, the revolt is dropped, and play resumes. (Clark's soldier on I14 and Silke's on Q24 are the two soldiers on the board.)")
taxes(.50, skip=(8,), collector="Clark, the Official, collects the tax for the crown")
say("Reina", "G21, beside the Summer Palace, so the crown keeps a palace for both seasons on the same row and nobody has to ask where the court is. Silke, Paddy: this palace is the foundation the coastal canals need, and you both know it. The order matters, and the crown builds in order.",
 "Reina: G21 keeps both palaces together, deep in Province 1 and well away from the edge squares Rob reached for last time. Every work the crown builds from now on stands on these two.")
build("Winter Palace", 20, {1:20}, key="Winter Palace", at="G21", text="Public works: Reina builds the Winter Palace on G21, at the crown's cost of 20.", hover="Reina pays the 20, into the Harvest Pile. The Winter Palace comes before the canals in Provinces 5 and 6.")
say("Rob", "Twenty to the Pile, Rich, so you'll be handing it back to me in harvests anyway. One new lad on M15, south of the Gentry. Rich, you've been paid twice for that Reservoir. I'd hate for anything to happen to the Bowl next to it.",
 "Rob: Clark's soldier is stuck guarding his own corner, and Rich hasn't got a soldier at all, so M15 sits a step or two from his Bowl on L14 and his Jar on M12. He sold my revolt twice, and now he can watch me walk up to his set.")
hire_soldier("M15", "Rob has no soldiers to march. He hires one for 20, paid to the Harvest Pile, and stands it on M15 in his desert, south of Rich's Province 3.")
salaries({2:15}, text="Salaries: Reina pays Clark 15 as the Official.")

# ===== CHAPTER 3: (title at the chapter's end)
# ============================== YEAR 5 ==============================
R(5, "Drought", "blight")
act("Reina turns the year's I Ching card: Drought. Every province collects an average harvest; no disasters. The population holds at x1, 100 million.")
say("Reina", "\"Drought. Not a cloud in months. The locusts, at least, are having a fine year.\" Even so, every province brings in an average harvest. The counter stays at one hundred million, the first multiple, so everyone reads the x1 row. Heaven has been dry before. The Empire is still standing.",
 "Reina: An average harvest in a drought year is about as kind as Heaven gets, and I can already hear the table calling fifty percent cruel. I won't let the weather set the crown's rate, and I want to see who is first to blame the dry sky on me.")
harvest("Rich, the Gentry, pays out the harvest from the Harvest Pile.")
say("Rich", "Paid in full from the Pile, every grain to the tenth, and in a drought year too. Silke, Paddy, the customary five and not a grain more. Heaven's dry enough without the landlord wringing you as well. Your Majesty, I trust the court will be just as gentle with the tax this year?",
 "Rich: Five years at five, and that's two peasants who'll remember whose door stayed open when the wells went dry. When the time comes to count friends instead of grain, I'd like them counting on me.")
rents(5, "Rents: Rich asks Silke and Paddy for the minimum rent, and each pays him 5.")
say("Reina", "Rich, the court is as gentle as it has always been: fifty, the same as last year and the four years before that. You paid out every grain to the tenth, so nobody here is starving, drought or no drought. And so the coast doesn't say the crown only builds for itself, Paddy, the first section of the Grand Canal goes into your province this year, at the crown's expense. Clark keeps his fifteen.",
 "Reina: The canal is my promise to the coast, made in my own time and on my own terms, and I'm not paying it as a ransom. Rich just asked to be bought again in front of everyone, so this year he gets nothing, and we'll see whether the man with the Reservoir dares to raise his hand.")
act("Reina declares a tax of 50% for the fifth year running: 17.5 each from Clark, Silke and Paddy and 27.5 from Rich, 80 in all, and 5 from Rob, who may refuse his. The crown will dig the first section of the Grand Canal in Paddy's Province 6, all 40 from the reserve (+10 to Province 6's harvest from next year), and pay Clark 15 at the year's end.", map_hl=[1,6])
